WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:16.540
When we first looked up to the stars, we couldn’t
help but imagine what might live there, yet,

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:20.410
so far, we have found nothing.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:26.700
When we first looked down into a microscope
though, well, we found more than we could

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.570
ever have guessed.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:35.980
Let’s pretend that you and I are among the
earliest viewers of microbes, peering through

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.480
a tiny piece of crafted glass set into a brass
plate.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:50.210
And, through that lens, we see a world that
is both entirely new, and unknowably ancient.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:56.440
And as we look, still in the earliest days
of charting this landscape, of cataloging

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:03.720
strange organisms that defy or blur the classifications
of plant or animal.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:11.610
We see, among those myriad, mysterious creatures—those bejeweled diatoms and charming rotifers that

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:21.630
make up our initial glimpses of the microcosmos—an
ensemble of blue or green trumpets: large

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:29.450
compared to many of the other organisms we
see, their elongated bodies adorned in rows

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.270
and rows of little hairs.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:40.549
It’s that horn shape that will end up identifying
them not just by sight, but by name: Stentor,

00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:47.780
for the herald of the Greek troops during
the Trojan War whose voice was said to carry

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.690
with the strength of fifty men.

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:56.119
Of course, our tools and understanding of
microbes have come a long way since those

00:01:56.119 --> 00:02:03.260
first microscopic investigations, making it
possible for us to share this footage of our

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.220
own observations with you.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:13.620
Recently, outside Warsaw, James found a small
pond in the middle of a forest that is filled

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.370
with microbes.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:21.760
That pond has given us more Stentors to collect
and film, which means it’s also provided

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:30.950
us with an excuse to check in again on our favorite under-appreciated organisms of the microcosmos.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:36.880
After all, Stentor’s voice carried through
millennia, but the legend of the microbial

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:44.099
Stentor seems more muted across the centuries
compared with some of the more studied ciliates.

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:50.220
But modern scientific tools are giving us
a new appreciation for Stentors.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.760
But before we get there, we should note that
it’s not like Stentors have never held any

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.130
fascination for scientists.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:03.890
As we talked about in our last Stentor-focused
episode, these single-celled organisms have

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:10.380
a complex body and an incredible capacity
for regeneration.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:16.970
That regeneration process mirrors some of
the steps that drive the development of animal

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:25.599
embryos, making Stentors a compelling object
of study all the way through the mid-20th century.

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:34.250
But research is as much about the practicalities
as it is about exciting questions and implications.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:41.060
And for many scientists, Stentors were simply
not the right choice for their experiments.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:46.790
They were difficult to grow, and, in part
because of their tremendous size, they couldn’t

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:53.050
be used in the same experimental set-ups that
other organisms were more amenable to.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:59.040
And so other ciliates that were easier to
work with, like paramecium, went on to become

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:08.300
more popular model protozoa, teaching us through
the lens of their own single-celled eukaryotic bodies.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:14.370
But Stentors weren’t forgotten, they were
just awaiting the right tools.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:22.030
We here work with the basics: forest ponds,
a microscope, and yes hundreds of hours spent

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:23.810
checking on our Stentors.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:29.139
And we still manage to find them in incredible
situations.

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:40.220
This little ciliate pulls and pulls at the
stentor’s membrane until it just…erupts.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:47.190
You can see the bead-like nuclei pouring out
of the Stentor along with the food vacuoles

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:52.811
and the rest of the cytoplasm, the inside
of its body now scattered around it.

00:04:52.811 --> 00:05:00.460
And yet…for all the damage that is inflicted
on this Stentor, it survives.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:06.990
It’s one thing to know that Stentors are
capable of this kind of regeneration, another

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:18.430
thing altogether to witness its survival in
real time on our slides.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:24.060
This regenerative capacity, their shape, their
color, their size, the fact that they are

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:31.240
single-celled organisms, all of those factors
make it seem like we must not have much in

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.940
common with Stentors.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:38.440
And if we were just reliant on what we could
see, we might still believe that.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:44.430
But with the ongoing development of new genetic
tools, it’s the thing we’ve discovered

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:50.449
that we share in common with Stentors, and
that actually renders them distinct from much

00:05:50.449 --> 00:06:01.949
of their brethren, that has given our trumpet
friends a new role in the study of the microscopic world.

00:06:01.949 --> 00:06:09.680
When it comes to genetics, we often think
of DNA as a universal code...this is the simple

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:12.910
story that we’re taught in biology class.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:18.750
Three base-pair sequences of DNA code for
amino acids to be added onto a protein over

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:25.410
and over until a gene ending code is reached
and the protein is complete.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:31.630
And those codes that decide what amino acid
is added onto the protein, well, we’re taught

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:34.759
that that is a universal thing.

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:36.520
It is not.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:44.030
There is what we call the standard code, the
one that we, and all multicellular organisms use.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:51.620
And then there are others where letters change,
and punctuation becomes words, and most of

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:55.729
these are bacterial or mitochondrial code.

00:06:55.729 --> 00:07:02.699
But especially among eukaryotes, ciliates
are noted genetic eccentrics due to their

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:09.860
tendency to take some of the sequences that,
in our code, signal the end of a gene, and

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:16.180
interpret them as coding for yet more amino
acids to stack onto the protein.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:23.370
Different ciliate lineages have evolved their
own versions of this altered interpretation,

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:30.039
but there are only a few ciliates that have
been thought to follow the standard genetic

00:07:30.039 --> 00:07:35.320
code that the rest of us do and Stentors are one of them.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:41.360
And with recent genetic techniques probing
the sequences of Stentor coeruleus, that strange

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.160
conformity has now been confirmed.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:52.380
Usually, when we bring up genetic studies
of microbes on this channel, they raise questions

00:07:52.389 --> 00:07:55.139
about the details of classification schemes.

00:07:55.139 --> 00:08:03.099
In the case of Stentors though, they raise
a deeper question: why do they resemble us

00:08:03.099 --> 00:08:09.150
in this way, and why don’t the vast majority
of other ciliates?

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:16.000
If Stentor coeruleus follows the standard
genetic code, does that mean the original

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.180
ciliate also did?

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:24.810
And if so, then does that mean the nonstandard
genetic codes exhibited by so many ciliates

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:32.909
now are not some property inherent to the
phylum, but a product of an evolutionary path

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:42.919
that started after the Stentor family began
to branch away, a very very long time ago?

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:45.949
Evolution is weird and messy.

00:08:45.949 --> 00:08:53.699
It is a knot that sometimes seems to become
more tangled the more we try to unravel it.

00:08:53.699 --> 00:09:00.759
And Stentors, well, they’re confirmation
that no matter how simple something may seem—whether

00:09:00.759 --> 00:09:07.439
it’s their own surprisingly complex single-celled
bodies or the supposed universality of the

00:09:07.439 --> 00:09:14.879
genetic code—it only takes a few “what’s”,
“why’s,” and “how’s” to find yourself

00:09:14.879 --> 00:09:23.670
enmeshed in a web of questions that is not
unlike the marvelous mysteries first uncovered

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:28.529
by the original microbe hunters hundreds of
years ago.

00:09:28.529 --> 00:09:35.110
Their discoveries were world changing, but
so are ours.

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:43.720
We live in a time of great knowing, of great
uncovering, but that has also helped us realize

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:49.230
how many mysteries we have yet to solve.

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:54.939
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:54.939 --> 00:10:00.400
If you would like to get a pin celebrating
the majesty of this peculiar organism, we

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.509
have Stentor coeruleus pins available now.

00:10:03.509 --> 00:10:05.769
There’s a link in the description.

00:10:05.769 --> 00:10:12.579
And also thank you so much to all of our patrons
on Patreon who allow us to continue these

00:10:12.579 --> 00:10:19.410
explorations and keep our eyes out for more
bizarre happenings in our microscopes.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:23.600
Without people like this, the work we’re
doing here on Journey to the Microcosmos would

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:24.970
not be possible.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:29.989
So if you’re able to potentially join them,
that would be amazing.

00:10:29.989 --> 00:10:34.089
And thank you to all of the people on this
list.

00:10:34.089 --> 00:10:39.079
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James, you can check out Jam

00:10:39.079 --> 00:10:40.700
&amp; Germ’s on Instagram.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:45.170
And if you want to see more from us, here
at Journey to the Microcosmos, you can find

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:49.320
us at youtube.com/microcosmos.

